@startuml How a combat unit gets its orders
' One game frame in the life of a fighting unit, with concrete classes.
' Not a UML spec: no aggregations, no cardinalities - just the order in
' which the pieces talk to each other, and where each decision lives.
' Reading order: top to bottom. The left side plans, the right side acts.

skinparam roundCorner 8
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 2
skinparam sequenceParticipantBorderThickness 1

title One frame of orders: Dragoon in the attack squad (Observer differs - see notes)

participant "Tactician\n(decides WHO fights)" as Tactician #E2EFDA
participant "SquadAttack\n(decides WHERE)" as Squad #E2EFDA
participant "Intention\n(order chit,\none per unit per frame)" as Intent #FFF2CC
participant "Agent\n(per-unit brain)" as Agent #DAEEF3
participant "FightOrFlee\n(should we fight?)" as FoF #DAEEF3
participant "Target\n(which enemy?)" as Target #DAEEF3
participant "Combat\n(how: Fight/Walk/Flee...)" as Combat #DAEEF3
participant "Commander\n(ONLY place that\ntouches the game)" as Commander #FCE4D6
participant "StarCraft\n(BWAPI)" as Game #F2F2F2

== Planning: intentions are written ==

Tactician -> Squad : run()\nassign freelancers
activate Squad
Squad -> Squad : chooseMode()\nHuntContact / ClearMap / ...
note right of Squad
  HuntContact latches its destination:
  freshest last-seen enemy first,
  then unseen tiles >= 16 tiles away.
  The latch stops followers (e.g. an
  Observer) flip-flopping every frame.
end note
Squad -> Intent : intend(tactic)\nterminus + targets
activate Intent
Intent --> Squad : same chit reused\nall frame (last writer wins)
deactivate Intent
deactivate Squad

== Micro: intentions become orders ==

Agent -> Agent : execute()\ncopy intent, tryDodge()
activate Agent
Agent -> FoF : shouldFight?
activate FoF
FoF --> Agent : yes / no (+ reason)
note right of FoF
  First matching rule wins.
  Pacifist: no weapon and no spells
  (Observer, Overlord...) never fights.
  It still follows (Walk) and flees.
end note
deactivate FoF
Agent -> Target : choose()\n(via matchups + scoring)
activate Target
Target --> Agent : target / none
deactivate Target
Agent -> Combat : perform()\npick technique
activate Combat
Combat --> Agent : act("Fight"/"Walk"/...)
note right of Combat
  Fight needs shouldFight + a target.
  Walk follows the terminus.
  Flee runs from threats.
end note
deactivate Combat

== Execution: exactly one BWAPI call ==

Combat -> Commander : attack() / move()\n...
activate Commander
Commander -> Game : bwapiUnit.attack()\nthe ONLY game call in the chain
deactivate Commander
deactivate Agent

note over Tactician, Game
  Observer example: same flow, but Pacifist forces
  Walk (never Fight), so it trails the army instead
  of approaching enemies it cannot harm.
  Its "Fight Move" tag meant: Fight technique + Move order.
end note

@enduml
